import sys
p = sys.argv[1]
lines = open(p, encoding='utf-8').read().split('\n')
idx = [i for i, l in enumerate(lines) if l.startswith('| 653 |')]
assert len(idx) == 1
lines.insert(idx[0] + 1, "| 654 | 2026-10-25 | **Floors and Home marks in plain grade names (her 08:17, 'the names are hard: just show the grade plainly'; then option 3, one floor per grade; 'Ground or Beginner? recommend' -> Beginner).** Built on row 653. (1) The house's storeys: 'FLOOR 1 · BEGINNER', 'FLOOR 2 · BEGINNER', 'FLOOR 3 · PRELIM – GRADE 1', 'FLOOR 4 · PRELIM – GRADE 1', 'FLOOR 5 · GRADES 2–3' (Korean '1층 · BEGINNER' ...). The floor number stays only while two storeys share a name: her option 3 makes one grade per floor, done floor by floor as each floor is rebuilt (the rooms of floors 3-5 each run across two grades today). floorName(k) gives the same words to the chapter end ('That was the last room on floor 2. Next: Floor 3 · Prelim – Grade 1.'), the Books shelf and the intros ('Start on floor 1, Beginner'); the books' small house prints the name on two lines when it is long. (2) The Home's marks in the same words: BEGINNER, PRELIM – GRADE 1, GRADES 2–3, GRADE 4, GRADE 5, Home only (row 653's I/II and 'P-1' are gone). Grade words stay in English in Korean too, as students say them. **Recommendation given:** 'Beginner' over 'Ground' (says what it is; 'Ground' needed explaining and the house's real ground floor is the hall). **Next:** the Beginner floor (Home groups, then the dollhouse), then Prelim and Grade 1 split into their own floors. Files: drills.html, drills.src.html, _workspace/concept-book-kit/sc/patch678.py. |")
open(p, 'w', encoding='utf-8').write('\n'.join(lines)); print('ok')
